In this lesson we'll look at how to design, test, and refine a spatial algorithm without getting stuck. Here's the cycle. First, design: write pseudocode that handles the typical case, just a clear plan, not real code yet. Then test: trace it by hand on simple inputs, and then push it with edge cases like equal points, horizontal or vertical pairs, right angles, or collinear points that form a degenerate shape. When something breaks, refine: fix the failing cases and simplify where you can. Then implement by turning your pseudocode into real code or a calculator program. Finally, justify: explain why each step works and what you're assuming. For a distance algorithm, test that same point gives zero, and check a far pair. For a triangle classifier, make sure equilateral, isosceles, scalene, and degenerate cases all come out right. And remember, every step needs a reason. Saying "average the x-coordinates" only works because the midpoint's x-coordinate is halfway between the two endpoints. Now let's see it in action.